I("gp","gp-osteoporosis-dexa","General Practice","Geriatrics","moderate","F",68,
 "the GP clinic","wrist fracture after a fall from standing, 20 years since menopause, BMI 19, never on HRT",
 "now healed, asking about bones",
 "Kyphosis mild, height loss 3 cm.",
 "DEXA T-score −2.7 at femoral neck. 25-OH vitamin D 40 nmol/L, Ca normal, eGFR 70.",
 "What is the most appropriate next step?",
 "Treat as osteoporosis: falls prevention, vitamin D/calcium as needed, and an antiresorptive (e.g. oral or IV bisphosphonate, or denosumab) with dental advice",
 ["Reassurance that a wrist fracture from standing is always 'just unlucky'","High-dose vitamin A","Oestrogen-progestogen HRT as the only option at 68 with a fracture","Ignore dental health before denosumab/bisphosphonate"],
 "A fragility fracture plus T-score ≤ −2.5 is osteoporosis. Treat the fracture risk, not only the T-score. Dental review reduces ONJ anxiety and risk.",
 ["Fragility fracture defines the disease.","Vitamin A is not the treatment.","HRT is not first-line at 68 for bones alone.","ONJ/atypical femur discussion is part of consent."],
 "A fracture from standing height is osteoporosis until investigated.","Stopping denosumab abruptly (rebound vertebral fractures).","Treat osteoporosis after a fragility fracture.","RACGP osteoporosis / Healthy Bones Australia"),

I("gp","gp-falls","General Practice","Geriatrics","moderate","M",84,
 "the GP clinic","two falls in 3 months, on prazosin, temazepam, and ramipril",
 "no syncope, no chest pain",
 "Postural drop 20 mmHg, vision poor, rugs at home, gait slow, no focal neurology.",
 "Hb 130, Na 136, ECG sinus, lying-standing BP positive.",
 "What is the most appropriate first approach?",
 "Multifactorial falls review: medicines (stop/reduce benzos and alpha-blockers if possible), vision, home hazards, strength and balance (e.g. Stepping On)",
 ["Immediate permanent bed rest","A routine head CT as the only intervention in a well clinic patient","Adding another sedative for 'anxiety about falling'","Ignoring medicines because falls are inevitable at 84"],
 "Falls are multifactorial. The highest-yield GP moves are medicine review and strength/balance programs.",
 ["Bed rest causes deconditioning.","Imaging if head injury/anticoagulation/focal signs — not as the only plan.","More sedation worsens falls.","Falls are not inevitable."],
 "Four or more medicines, benzos, and postural hypotension are red flags.","Restarting prazosin 'for sleep' after a fall.","Do a multifactorial falls review.","RACGP / ACSQHC falls guidelines"),

I("gp","gp-uti-uncomplicated","General Practice","Common presentations","moderate","F",27,
 "the GP clinic","sexually active, no pregnancy, no loin pain",
 "24 hours of dysuria and frequency",
 "Afebrile, no loin tenderness, abdomen soft. Pregnancy test negative.",
 "Dipstick: nitrites and leucocytes positive.",
 "What is the most appropriate management?",
 "A short course of a recommended empirical antibiotic (e.g. trimethoprim or nitrofurantoin as per local resistance) and advice on red flags",
 ["Ciprofloxacin as first-line in all simple cystitis in Australia","No urine assessment and 14 days of IV antibiotics","Imaging of the kidneys in all simple first episodes","Methenamine as emergency treatment of sepsis"],
 "Uncomplicated cystitis in a non-pregnant woman: short-course oral antibiotics per Therapeutic Guidelines; reserve quinolones.",
 ["Quinolones are not first-line for simple UTI in Australia.","She is not septic.","Imaging is for recurrent, male, febrile, or complicated UTI.","Not septic."],
 "Always exclude pregnancy.","Using ciprofloxacin 'because it is strong'.","Treat uncomplicated cystitis with a short oral course.","Therapeutic Guidelines"),

I("gp","gp-sinusitis-abx","General Practice","Common presentations","moderate","M",34,
 "the GP clinic","5 days of viral URTI",
 "facial pressure, green mucus, still working, no fever now",
 "Tender over sinuses, no periorbital swelling, no diplopia, teeth not the source.",
 "No imaging indicated.",
 "What is the most appropriate management?",
 "Analgesia, saline, intranasal corticosteroid if prolonged; antibiotics are not routinely indicated before 10 days unless severe/unilateral/systemic features",
 ["Immediate amoxicillin–clavulanate in all 5-day viral rhinosinusitis","CT in all primary-care viral sinusitis","Oral decongestants for 6 weeks","Urgent craniotomy"],
 "Most rhinosinusitis is viral. Antibiotics do not help early uncomplicated disease and drive resistance.",
 ["5 days of green mucus is not a mandate for antibiotics.","Imaging is for complications.","Topical decongestants should be short-term (rhinitis medicamentosa).","No orbital/neuro complication."],
 "Double sickening, 10 days, or red flags change the decision.","Antibiotics 'to be on the safe side' at day 3.","Do not routinely antibiotic-treat early sinusitis.","Therapeutic Guidelines / RACGP"),

I("gp","gp-sore-throat","General Practice","Common presentations","moderate","F",19,
 "the GP clinic","no cough, fever, tender anterior nodes, exudate",
 "sore throat for 2 days",
 "Temperature 38.3°C, exudative tonsils, tender cervical nodes, no rash, no airway threat.",
 "Centor/McIsaac high. Rapid strep not always available. EBV possible but less typical without fatigue/spleen.",
 "What is the most appropriate antibiotic decision in Australian primary care?",
 "Consider phenoxymethylpenicillin if bacterial tonsillitis is likely (Centor/FeverPAIN), but remember most adult sore throats are viral",
 ["Amoxicillin as preferred if EBV has not been excluded (rash risk)","Immediate tonsillectomy","Ciprofloxacin","No analgesia because antibiotics will remove pain in 30 minutes"],
 "Australian practice is more conservative than US strep-culture culture. Penicillin V if treating; avoid amoxicillin if EBV is possible.",
 ["Amoxicillin + EBV = rash.","Surgery is not acute care.","Wrong spectrum.","Analgesia is the main symptomatic treatment."],
 "Airway, epiglottitis, and quinsy are the emergencies — not the culture swab.","Prescribing amoxicillin in a kissing-age adult with fatigue and a grey membrane.","Use penicillin V if treating bacterial tonsillitis.","Therapeutic Guidelines"),

I("gp","gp-back-red-flags","General Practice","Common presentations","moderate","M",61,
 "the GP clinic","new thoracic back pain, history of prostate cancer 3 years ago",
 "night pain, unexplained weight loss, not improving at rest",
 "Percussion tenderness over T8, no neurology yet, no fever.",
 "PSA 45 (previously undetectable). XR pending.",
 "What is the most appropriate next step?",
 "Urgent investigation for metastatic cord/vertebral disease (imaging and oncology/spinal discussion), not routine 'mechanical back pain' physiotherapy alone",
 ["Reassurance and 6 weeks of rest without investigation","Manipulation under anaesthesia in GP","Immediate high-dose NSAID as the only action","Chiropractic as sufficient for cancer-related night pain"],
 "Red flags: history of cancer, night pain, weight loss, thoracic pain, neurology, infection signs, trauma, steroid use, IVDU. Investigate.",
 ["Red flags override the usual 6-week wait.","Not a GP procedure.","NSAIDs may mask.","Do not manipulate possible metastases."],
 "Thoracic pain is a red flag more often than lumbar.","Calling all back pain 'muscular' in a cancer survivor.","Investigate red-flag back pain urgently.","RACGP / NSW ACI back-pain guidance"),

I("gp","gp-knee-oa","General Practice","Chronic disease","moderate","F",64,
 "the GP clinic","BMI 33, medial knee pain worse with stairs, 2 years",
 "no locking, no fever",
 "Bony enlargement, crepitus, no effusion of note, cool joint.",
 "XR: medial joint-space narrowing, osteophytes.",
 "What is the most appropriate first-line management?",
 "Weight loss, exercise (including strength), and topical or oral NSAIDs as tolerated; surgery if refractory",
 ["Immediate total knee replacement in all Kellgren grade 2","Long-term oral glucocorticoids as first-line OA therapy","Antibiotics","Bed rest for 3 months"],
 "OA is a clinical and radiographic diagnosis. NICE/RACGP: education, exercise, weight loss first; NSAIDs; injections; then surgery.",
 ["Surgery is for failed conservative care or severe disease.","Chronic oral steroids are not OA treatment.","Not septic.","Rest worsens deconditioning."],
 "Weight loss of 10% can transform knee OA.","Jumping to arthroscopic washout (not effective for OA).","Start with weight loss and exercise in knee OA.","RACGP OA guideline"),

I("gp","gp-gout-acute","General Practice","Common presentations","moderate","M",55,
 "the GP clinic","beer, diuretic for hypertension, previous podagra",
 "exquisite pain in the right first MTP overnight",
 "Hot, red, swollen 1st MTP, afebrile, no wound.",
 "Urate 0.48 mmol/L (may be normal in an attack). eGFR 70. Not on allopurinol yet.",
 "What is the most appropriate acute treatment if no contraindication?",
 "NSAIDs, colchicine, or a short corticosteroid course for the acute attack; do not start allopurinol during the peak of an untreated attack without cover",
 ["Allopurinol as monotherapy of the acute attack","Antibiotics for all podagra","Immediate joint replacement","High-dose aspirin as urate-lowering"],
 "Acute gout: anti-inflammatories. Urate-lowering (allopurinol) is for recurrent disease and is started with anti-inflammatory cover, not as the sole acute drug.",
 ["Allopurinol can worsen an acute attack if started naked.","Cellulitis is the differential — but this is classic podagra.","Not a surgical disease acutely.","Aspirin is not the treatment."],
 "Normal serum urate does not exclude an acute attack.","Stopping allopurinol during an attack if already established on it.","Treat acute gout with anti-inflammatories, not allopurinol alone.","Therapeutic Guidelines"),

I("gp","gp-asthma-preventer","General Practice","Chronic disease","moderate","F",29,
 "the GP clinic","uses salbutamol 3–4 times a week, wakes at night monthly",
 "wheeze with exercise",
 "Peak flow 80% predicted, not currently distressed, no clubbing.",
 "No spirometry on file for 3 years.",
 "What is the most appropriate step-up?",
 "Add an inhaled corticosteroid-containing preventer (or MART with ICS-formoterol as per Australian Asthma Handbook) rather than SABA-only",
 ["Continue SABA-only indefinitely","Oral prednisolone as maintenance in mild asthma","A LABA inhaler without ICS","Antibiotics for all exercise wheeze"],
 "SABA-only is no longer acceptable if symptoms are this frequent. ICS or ICS-formoterol MART reduces exacerbations.",
 ["Overuse of SABA is a mortality risk.","Oral steroids are for exacerbations.","LABA without ICS is dangerous in asthma.","This is not infection."],
 "Ask how many salbutamol inhalers they collect a year.","A blue puffer only in someone waking at night.","Do not leave frequent asthma on SABA-only.","Australian Asthma Handbook"),

I("gp","gp-copd-exacerbation-gp","General Practice","Chronic disease","moderate","M",69,
 "the GP clinic","40-pack-year, known COPD, saturations usually 92%",
 "3 days of increased dyspnoea, purulent sputum, no confusion",
 "RR 22, sats 90% on air, no new oedema, afebrile now, speaking sentences.",
 "CXR no pneumothorax or new consolidation of pneumonia. ABG not available in GP.",
 "What is the most appropriate GP management if he is safe for community care?",
 "Increase bronchodilators, a short course of prednisolone, and antibiotics if sputum is purulent, with a clear safety-net for hospital",
 ["Long-term high-dose oral steroids as the only change","Home oxygen to 100% without checking CO2 retention history","Immediate NIV in the GP carpark","No steroids in COPD exacerbations ever"],
 "COPD exacerbation: bronchodilators, 5 days of prednisolone, antibiotics if infective phenotype, and risk-stratify for hospital (acidosis, hypoxia, frailty, pneumonia).",
 ["Chronic oral steroids cause harm.","Uncontrolled O2 can worsen hypercapnia.","NIV is a hospital therapy.","Steroids help exacerbations."],
 "The 5-day steroid course is enough for many — do not leave them on 25 mg for months.","Sending a CO2 retainer home on high-flow O2 without a plan.","Treat COPD exacerbations with steroids ± antibiotics.","COPD-X / Therapeutic Guidelines"),

I("gp","gp-allergic-rhinitis","General Practice","Common presentations","moderate","F",22,
 "the GP clinic","seasonal sneezing, itchy eyes, clear rhinorrhoea",
 "exam season, poor sleep from nasal obstruction",
 "Pale boggy turbinates, allergic shiners, no polyps of concern, chest clear.",
 "Not overusing topical decongestants yet.",
 "What is the most effective regular treatment?",
 "Intranasal corticosteroid spray, with an antihistamine if needed, and allergen measures",
 ["Oral prednisolone as first-line every spring","Antibiotics","Topical decongestant for 3 months","No treatment because it is 'just hay fever'"],
 "Allergic rhinitis: intranasal steroids are the most effective maintenance. Oral antihistamines help itch/sneeze more than blockage.",
 ["Systemic steroids are not routine.","Not bacterial sinusitis.","Decongestants >3–5 days cause rhinitis medicamentosa.","It impairs sleep and asthma control."],
 "Aim the spray out, not at the septum.","Months of oxymetazoline.","Use intranasal steroids for allergic rhinitis.","ASCIA / Therapeutic Guidelines"),

I("gp","gp-eczema-child-gp","General Practice","Common presentations","moderate","F",2,
 "the GP clinic","flexural itch, family atopy",
 "keeping the family awake, no fever",
 "Flexural eczema, dry skin, no pustules of infection, growth normal.",
 "No investigation required for typical atopic eczema.",
 "What is the most appropriate baseline treatment?",
 "Liberal emollients plus intermittent topical corticosteroid of appropriate strength to inflamed skin, and education about steroid safety",
 ["Antibiotics as first-line for all eczema","Avoiding all topical steroids because of 'skin thinning' in every toddler","Oral prednisolone as first-line for mild flexural eczema","Exclusion of all foods without dietetic support as first-line"],
 "Eczema: moisturise, anti-inflammatory topicals, treat infection if present, and do not under-treat with steroid phobia.",
 ["Infection is not always present.","Appropriate TCS is safe and prevents chronic damage.","Oral steroids are for severe flares.","Empirical broad exclusion diets are harmful."],
 "Use enough steroid to get control, then step down.","Hydrocortisone 1% forever on thick lichenified plaques.","Emollients plus topical steroids for eczema.","ASCIA / RCH"),

I("gp","gp-acne-isotretinoin","General Practice","Common presentations","moderate","M",18,
 "the GP clinic","nodulocystic acne, scarring, failed 6 months of topical plus oral antibiotics",
 "low mood screened, not suicidal",
 "Scarring cystic acne on face and back.",
 "LFTs and lipids would be needed if isotretinoin is started. Pregnancy N/A.",
 "What is the most appropriate next treatment consideration?",
 "Referral for isotretinoin (or GP shared-care where permitted), after discussing teratogenicity, mood, and bloods",
 ["Lifelong rotating antibiotics without considering isotretinoin in scarring acne","Prednisolone as sole long-term acne therapy","Reassurance that scarring will reverse with face wash","Roaccutane bought online without monitoring"],
 "Scarring nodulocystic acne is an isotretinoin indication. Antibiotics should not be endless. Pregnancy prevention is critical in people who can become pregnant.",
 ["Antibiotic stewardship and scarring risk.","Steroids are not long-term acne care.","Scars are preventable now.","Unmonitored online isotretinoin is dangerous."],
 "Treat scarring acne early — scars are for life.","Three years of minocycline (pigment, autoimmune hepatitis).","Refer scarring acne for isotretinoin.","Australasian College of Dermatologists"),

I("gp","gp-iron-deficiency","General Practice","Common presentations","moderate","F",45,
 "the GP clinic","menorrhagia, fatigue, vegetarian",
 "no melaena, no weight loss, no postmenopausal bleeding",
 "Pallor, no abdominal mass, pelvic exam deferred to gynae if needed.",
 "Hb 88, MCV 68, ferritin 6, coeliac serology negative. She is premenopausal.",
 "What is the most appropriate dual plan?",
 "Iron replacement and investigation/treatment of menorrhagia (and GI work-up if atypical features)",
 ["Iron without ever asking about periods or diet","Colonoscopy as absolutely mandatory before any iron in all 45-year-old women with heavy periods and no red flags","Transfusion as first-line in a stable Hb 88","B12 as the treatment of low ferritin"],
 "Premenopausal iron deficiency is often menstrual plus diet. Replace iron and fix the source. GI investigation is more mandatory in men and postmenopausal women.",
 ["Always seek a source.","GI work-up is still considered if red flags or failure to respond.","Transfuse for instability or very severe anaemia.","Ferritin is iron, not B12."],
 "Ferritin is the test; a normal Hb does not exclude iron deficiency.","Giving iron and missing coeliac or colon cancer in a man.","Replace iron and treat the cause.","Gastroenterological Society of Australia / RACGP"),

I("gp","gp-b12-metformin","General Practice","Chronic disease","moderate","M",68,
 "the GP clinic","type 2 diabetes on metformin for 12 years, vegan for 3 years",
 "paraesthesiae of the feet, glossitis, mild memory change",
 "Reduced vibration at toes, pale, no UMN signs of severe SCD yet.",
 "Hb 101, MCV 110, B12 low, folate normal, anti-IF antibodies pending.",
 "What is the most appropriate treatment?",
 "Vitamin B12 replacement (IM if symptomatic deficiency/neurology, or high-dose oral in selected cases) and review metformin/diet",
 ["Folate alone before B12 in combined deficiency risk","Iron as the treatment of macrocytosis with low B12","No replacement because metformin 'always' explains a low B12 without treatment","Folic acid 5 mg as the only therapy of neurological B12 deficiency"],
 "B12 deficiency from pernicious anaemia, diet, or metformin needs replacement. Give B12 before folate if both are low to avoid precipitating subacute combined degeneration.",
 ["Folate first can worsen neurology.","Iron treats microcytosis.","Low B12 still needs treatment.","Folate is not the neurological treatment."],
 "Check B12 in unexplained neuropathy and in long-term metformin.","Folic acid for a high MCV without checking B12.","Replace B12 in deficiency, especially with neurology.","Therapeutic Guidelines"),

I("gp","gp-hypothyroid","General Practice","Chronic disease","moderate","F",41,
 "the GP clinic","fatigue, weight gain, menorrhagia, cold intolerance",
 "tiredness and heavy periods for 6 months",
 "Slow relaxing reflexes, small goitre, no orbitopathy.",
 "TSH 28 mU/L, free T4 low, anti-TPO positive.",
 "What is the most likely diagnosis and treatment?",
 "Primary hypothyroidism due to Hashimoto thyroiditis; start levothyroxine and titrate to TSH",
 ["Hyperthyroidism","Liothyronine as routine first-line monotherapy in Australia","Carbimazole","Iodine-131 as first-line for hypothyroidism"],
 "High TSH, low T4, TPO antibodies: Hashimoto. Replace with T4. Recheck TSH in 6–8 weeks.",
 ["TSH is high, not low.","T3 monotherapy is not standard first-line.","Carbimazole treats hyperthyroidism.","Radioiodine is for hyperthyroidism."],
 "Do not start high-dose T4 in the frail or in possible ischaemic heart disease.","Treating a TSH of 4.8 in a well young woman as an emergency.","Replace T4 in primary hypothyroidism.","Endocrine Society of Australia"),

I("gp","gp-fatigue","General Practice","Common presentations","moderate","F",36,
 "the GP clinic","6 months of tiredness, still working",
 "no weight loss, no fever, sleeps 5 hours with a toddler and a phone in bed",
 "Examination normal, no nodes, no goitre, BMI 24.",
 "FBC, ferritin, TSH, EUC, glucose, coeliac serology, CRP normal. EPDS 6. Sleep hygiene poor.",
 "What is the most appropriate next step?",
 "A structured sleep, mood, and psychosocial review rather than endless rare-disease tests after a sensible screen is negative",
 ["PET scan as first-line for fatigue with a normal examination","Prednisolone trial for 'adrenal fatigue' as a recognised evidence-based entity","Ignoring depression screening forever","Repeating the whole panel weekly for 6 months"],
 "Fatigue is common. After a focused screen, the yield of more tests is low; sleep, mood, iron, thyroid, and life load dominate.",
 ["Imaging needs a clue.","'Adrenal fatigue' is not a valid diagnosis; true adrenal insufficiency looks different.","Always screen mood.","Repeating normal tests wastes and alarms."],
 "Ask how many hours they actually sleep.","Ordering a whole-body CT for tiredness with a normal exam.","Screen common causes of fatigue, then address sleep and mood.","RACGP"),

I("gp","gp-headache-gp","General Practice","Common presentations","moderate","F",31,
 "the GP clinic","recurrent unilateral throbbing headache with photophobia and nausea, 8 per month, normal between attacks",
 "no thunderclap, no fever, no neurological deficit",
 "Examination including fundi normal. Not pregnant.",
 "No imaging required for typical migraine with a normal exam.",
 "What is the most appropriate management?",
 "Acute: triptan plus NSAID/antiemetic as needed; preventer if ≥4–5 disabling days a month (e.g. propranolol, amitriptyline, candesartan, or CGRP pathway as indicated)",
 ["Opioids as first-line migraine treatment","CT for every typical migraine in GP","Prednisolone as acute treatment of all migraine","Reassurance that 8 days a month never warrants prevention"],
 "Migraine is clinical. Avoid opioids. Offer prevention when attacks are frequent or disabling. Medication-overuse headache is the trap.",
 ["Opioids worsen chronic headache.","Imaging if red flags.","Steroids are not standard acute migraine care.","Eight days a month is a preventer conversation."],
 "Limit acute treatments to avoid MOH.","Codeine for migraine for a decade.","Treat migraine with triptans/NSAIDs and consider prevention.","ANZAN / Therapeutic Guidelines"),

I("gp","gp-dv-screening","General Practice","Women's health","moderate","F",34,
 "the GP clinic","vague abdominal pain, third visit this month, partner answers for her",
 "bruises of different ages on upper arms, delayed presentation",
 "Partner refuses to leave the room.",
 "No acute abdomen. Pregnancy test negative today.",
 "What is the most appropriate GP action?",
 "Create a chance to see her alone, ask about family violence, assess safety, and offer specialist services — do not ignore the pattern",
 ["Demand she leave tonight as the only acceptable outcome in the room with the partner present","Ignore bruises because it is 'none of our business'","Call the partner in to 'mediate' immediately","A pregnancy test as the only action ever needed"],
 "Family violence is a core GP issue. See the patient alone, use validated questions, safety-plan, and document. Mandatory reporting applies to children at risk.",
 ["Leaving is a process; forcing it in front of the perpetrator can increase danger.","It is our business.","Mediation with the perpetrator is unsafe.","Always consider pregnancy, but it is not the whole task."],
 "The most dangerous time is when she tries to leave.","Writing 'clumsy' for patterned bruises.","Ask about family violence when you see the pattern.","RACGP White Book"),

I("gp","gp-smoking-cessation","General Practice","Prevention","moderate","M",49,
 "the GP clinic","25-pack-year, wants to stop, first cigarette within 15 minutes of waking",
 "motivated after a friend's MI",
 "Chest clear, BP 142/88.",
 "He has failed 'cold turkey' twice.",
 "What is the most effective GP approach?",
 "Brief advice plus combination NRT or varenicline (as currently available) plus behavioural support — pharmacotherapy plus support beats either alone",
 ["E-cigarettes as the only RACGP-endorsed first-line in all patients without discussing licensed medicines","Telling him willpower is the only method","Bupropion in uncontrolled seizure disorder as first-line","No follow-up because advice was given"],
 "Ask, Advise, Help. Combination NRT or varenicline plus behavioural support has the best quit rates. Follow up.",
 ["Licensed medicines remain first-line; e-cigarettes are a separate, regulated discussion.","Willpower alone has poor rates.","Bupropion lowers seizure threshold.","Follow-up doubles success."],
 "Time to first cigarette measures dependence.","A 2-minute 'you should stop' without a plan.","Offer pharmacotherapy plus support for smoking cessation.","RACGP smoking guidance"),

I("gp","gp-alcohol-audit","General Practice","Prevention","moderate","F",46,
 "the GP clinic","drinks a bottle of wine most nights, MCV 101, GGT 90",
 "insomnia, relationship strain",
 "No withdrawal tremor today. Liver not enlarged.",
 "AUDIT-C high. FBC: MCV 101. USS not yet indicated.",
 "What is the most appropriate GP intervention?",
 "A brief intervention using AUDIT, agreed reduction or abstinence goals, thiamine if dependent, and follow-up; refer if dependent or failed brief advice",
 ["Ignoring alcohol because LFTs are 'only a bit up'","Disulfiram on the spot without consent or support","Normalising a bottle of wine nightly as 'Mediterranean'","CT liver as the first conversation"],
 "GP brief interventions reduce consumption in hazardous drinkers. Dependent drinkers need withdrawal planning and specialist support.",
 ["MCV and GGT are clues, not reassurance.","Disulfiram needs a plan.","A bottle a night is 7–8 standard drinks.","Conversation before imaging."],
 "One standard drink is 10 g alcohol in Australia — a bottle of wine is not 'two drinks'.","Writing 'social drinker' for a bottle a night.","Use brief intervention and AUDIT in hazardous drinking.","NHMRC alcohol guidelines"),

I("gp","gp-obesity","General Practice","Chronic disease","moderate","M",41,
 "the GP clinic","BMI 38, pre-diabetes, knee OA, wants help",
 "failed diets, no endocrine red flags",
 "BP 148/90, waist 124 cm, no striae of Cushing.",
 "HbA1c 6.3%, TSH normal.",
 "What is the most appropriate framework?",
 "A multicomponent program: diet, activity, sleep, and consideration of PBS-eligible medicines or bariatric referral if criteria met — not stigma",
 ["A single lecture on willpower as sufficient","Thyroidectomy with a normal TSH","Orlistat as mandatory in all before any diet discussion","Reassurance that BMI 38 has no health impact"],
 "Obesity is a chronic disease. Use 5–10% weight-loss targets, medicines when indicated, and bariatric surgery for eligible high-BMI patients with comorbidity.",
 ["Stigma worsens outcomes.","TSH is normal.","Medicines adjunct, not a substitute for a plan.","BMI 38 is high risk."],
 "Ask permission, then ask what they have already tried.","A BMI joke in the waiting room.","Treat obesity as a chronic disease with a multimodal plan.","Australian obesity guidelines"),

I("gp","gp-contraception-cocp","General Practice","Women's health","moderate","F",33,
 "the GP clinic","requests the pill, smokes 15/day, migraine with aura, BMI 32",
 "needs contraception",
 "BP 138/86. No DVT history.",
 "She has used a COCP before without issue.",
 "What is the most appropriate advice?",
 "Combined hormonal contraception is contraindicated with migraine with aura (stroke risk); offer progestogen-only methods or an IUD",
 ["A 50-microgram ethinylestradiol pill as safer in aura","No contraception needed at 33","Copper IUD as contraindicated in all smokers","Depo as the only legal option"],
 "UKMEC/Australian: migraine with aura is a contraindication to combined hormones. Smoking over 35 is another. IUDs and POP/implant are alternatives.",
 ["Higher oestrogen is worse.","Fertility is intact.","Copper IUD is fine in smokers.","Several options exist."],
 "Aura + combined hormones is a stroke conversation.","Restarting the pill after a TIA because 'it helps the skin'.","Do not use combined hormones with migraine with aura.","FSRH / Australian contraceptive guidelines"),

I("gp","gp-menopause-hrt","General Practice","Women's health","moderate","F",51,
 "the GP clinic","hot flushes, night sweats, last period 14 months ago, no contraindications",
 "poor sleep, irritable, vaginal dryness",
 "BP 128/80, breasts normal, smear up to date.",
 "She has no unexplained PV bleeding. Cardiovascular risk is not high.",
 "What is the most appropriate treatment for vasomotor symptoms if she wants medicines?",
 "Menopausal hormone therapy (oestrogen plus a progestogen because she has a uterus), after discussing VTE/breast-cancer risk",
 ["Unopposed oestrogen with an intact uterus","Testosterone as first-line for flushes","Clonidine as more effective than MHT for most women","Vaginal oestrogen as sufficient for systemic flushes alone"],
 "MHT is the most effective treatment for vasomotor symptoms. Women with a uterus need endometrial protection. Vaginal oestrogen treats GSM locally.",
 ["Unopposed oestrogen causes endometrial hyperplasia/cancer.","Testosterone is for selected hypoactive desire, not flushes.","Clonidine is a modest non-hormonal option.","Local oestrogen does not treat night sweats well."],
 "Start MHT within 10 years of menopause for the best risk–benefit.","Unopposed oestrogen in a woman with a uterus.","Use combined MHT for flushes if the uterus is in situ.","RANZCOG / AMS / RACGP"),

I("gp","gp-antenatal-shared","General Practice","Women's health","easy","F",29,
 "the GP clinic","positive home pregnancy test, 6+2 by LMP, wanted pregnancy",
 "nausea, no pain, no bleeding",
 "BP 110/70, uterus not yet palpable.",
 "Urine no protein, anti-D status unknown, serology and dating scan to arrange.",
 "What is the most appropriate next step?",
 "Confirm intrauterine pregnancy, offer dating scan, antenatal bloods (including blood group/antibodies, viruses, FBC, ferritin), folate/iodine, and a shared-care plan",
 ["Live vaccines (MMR) now that she is pregnant","ACE inhibitor for 'prevention'","CT abdomen to date the pregnancy","Telling her to stop all exercise including walking"],
 "First visit: confirm dates, screen, start folate 400 micrograms (higher if indicated), iodine, influenza/pertussis later, and refer as per risk.",
 ["MMR is contraindicated in pregnancy — give postpartum if non-immune.","ACE inhibitors are teratogenic.","Ultrasound, not CT.","Walking is encouraged."],
 "High-dose folate if BMI ≥30, diabetes, or previous NTD.","Forgetting anti-D group.","Do a complete first antenatal screen.","RANZCOG / Australian pregnancy care guidelines"),

I("gp","gp-child-imms","General Practice","Vaccination","easy","M",0,
 "the GP clinic","6-week check, well",
 "due immunisations, parents anxious about 'too many needles'",
 "Thriving, fontanelle soft, red reflex present, hips stable.",
 "Australian Immunisation Register shows no doses yet.",
 "What is the most appropriate advice?",
 "Recommend the NIP 6-week vaccines today; combination vaccines are safe and delay increases disease risk",
 ["Delay all vaccines until 2 years to 'protect the immune system'","Give MMR at 6 weeks as per NIP","No pertussis-containing vaccine in infancy","Antibiotics instead of immunisation"],
 "The NIP 6-week visit is hexavalent, pneumococcal, and rotavirus (as scheduled). Combination vaccines do not 'overload' the immune system.",
 ["Delay leaves infants unprotected from pertussis and rotavirus.","MMR is at 12 months.","Pertussis protection is a key reason for 6-week vaccines.","Antibiotics are not a substitute."],
 "Maternal pertussis vaccination in pregnancy protects the first weeks.","Spreading out vaccines without a catch-up plan.","Give NIP vaccines on time at 6 weeks.","Australian Immunisation Handbook", age_label="6-week-old"),

I("gp","gp-impetigo","General Practice","Common presentations","moderate","M",4,
 "the GP clinic","crusted golden sores on the face, sibling affected",
 "well, afebrile, no cellulitis",
 "Honey-coloured crusts around the mouth, no orbital involvement.",
 "School exclusion until 24 hours of antibiotics (or as per local public-health advice).",
 "What is the most appropriate treatment?",
 "Topical mupirocin or fusidic acid for localised non-bullous impetigo, or oral flucloxacillin if extensive, plus hygiene and exclusion",
 ["Oral prednisolone","No exclusion from daycare","Aciclovir as first-line for golden crusts","Methotrexate"],
 "Impetigo is S. aureus/S. pyogenes. Treat, cover, and exclude to protect the daycare. Rising resistance means do not use topical antibiotics casually for every scratch.",
 ["Not an inflammatory dermatitis primarily.","Exclusion matters.","HSV is painful grouped vesicles.","Not an immunosuppressive disease."],
 "Treat household contacts' sores too.","Sending a crusted child back to daycare the same morning.","Treat impetigo and follow exclusion rules.","Therapeutic Guidelines / NHMRC Staying Healthy"),

I("gp","gp-scabies","General Practice","Common presentations","moderate","F",20,
 "the GP clinic","share house, intense itch worse at night, burrows in finger webs",
 "housemates itchy too",
 "Burrows, excoriations, no fever.",
 "No investigation required if classic.",
 "What is the most appropriate management?",
 "Permethrin 5% to the whole body (as directed), repeat at 1 week, treat contacts, and wash linen",
 ["Steroid cream as sole therapy of scabies","Antibiotics as first-line without treating mites","Ivermectin as banned in all Australian scabies","Ignoring contacts"],
 "Scabies: permethrin (or oral ivermectin in selected/crusted cases), treat all close contacts simultaneously, and hot-wash linen.",
 ["Steroids ease itch but do not kill mites.","Secondary infection may need antibiotics but treat the scabies.","Ivermectin is used for crusted/institutional outbreaks.","Contacts will rebound the infestation."],
 "Itch can persist 2 weeks after successful treatment.","Treating only the index case in a share house.","Treat scabies and all close contacts together.","Therapeutic Guidelines"),

I("gp","gp-travel-yellow-fever","General Practice","Prevention","moderate","F",35,
 "the GP clinic","travelling to rural Ghana in 6 weeks, no prior yellow-fever vaccine",
 "wants vaccines",
 "Well, not pregnant, not thymectomised, not severely egg-allergic in a way that precludes discussion.",
 "Routine NIP is up to date. She will be in rural West Africa in wet season.",
 "What additional vaccine is a regulatory and clinical issue for this itinerary?",
 "Yellow fever vaccine at an approved centre (and documentation for entry), plus malaria chemoprophylaxis advice",
 ["No malaria discussion for rural Ghana","Live yellow fever vaccine in a severely immunocompromised traveller as routine without specialist advice","Cholera vaccine as the only vaccine ever needed for Ghana","Influenza vaccine as a substitute for yellow fever"],
 "West Africa: yellow fever (often required), hepatitis A, typhoid, routine boosters, and malaria prevention. Use an approved yellow-fever centre.",
 ["Malaria is essential.","Live vaccines in the immunocompromised need specialist care.","Cholera is not the main risk for most travellers.","Different diseases."],
 "Start travel consults 6 weeks out.","A last-minute yellow-fever jab the day before departure without a plan.","Offer yellow fever vaccine and malaria prevention for rural West Africa.","Australian Immunisation Handbook / CDC-aligned travel"),

I("gp","gp-melanoma","General Practice","Common presentations","moderate","M",58,
 "the GP clinic","new black irregular back lesion, changing over 3 months, fair skin, previous sunburns",
 "no itch, no bleed yet",
 "Asymmetry, irregular border, colour variation, 8 mm, ugly duckling.",
 "No regional nodes. Dermoscopy atypical if available.",
 "What is the most appropriate next step?",
 "Urgent diagnostic excision with appropriate margins (or rapid dermatology) — do not shave-biopsy a suspected melanoma if complete excision is feasible",
 ["Reassurance and review in 12 months","Cryotherapy of a suspected melanoma","Punch biopsy of the edge as always equivalent to complete excision in suspected melanoma","Sunscreen as the only action for a changing 8 mm black lesion"],
 "ABCDE plus ugly duckling. Complete excision biopsy is preferred for suspected melanoma. Do not freeze first.",
 ["Changing pigmented lesions need a diagnosis.","Cryo destroys histology.","Partial biopsy can miss the diagnostic area.","Prevention is too late for this lesion."],
 "If it looks like a melanoma, cut it out — don't watch it for a year.","Shave biopsy of a thick melanoma that then cannot be staged well.","Excise suspected melanoma for diagnosis.","Cancer Council / ACD"),

I("gp","gp-hearing","General Practice","Geriatrics","moderate","F",77,
 "the GP clinic","family say the TV is loud, she denies a problem",
 "social withdrawal, not 'confused' on MMSE",
 "Whisper test failed, canals clear, drums normal.",
 "No sudden unilateral loss (that would be urgent). Audiology indicated.",
 "What is the most appropriate next step?",
 "Refer for audiology and consider hearing aids; treat wax if present; sudden unilateral loss is an ENT emergency — this is gradual",
 ["Immediate high-dose steroids for bilateral gradual presbycusis without ENT advice","Reassurance that hearing loss is not worth treating at 77","A cochlear implant as first-line in mild presbycusis","Gentamicin ear drops 'just in case'"],
 "Presbycusis is common and treatable. Hearing loss is linked to cognitive decline and isolation. Sudden sensorineural loss is a different, urgent pathway.",
 ["Steroids are for sudden SNHL under specialist care.","Treatment improves quality of life.","Implants are for selected severe loss.","Aminoglycosides are ototoxic."],
 "Whisper test then audiology.","Calling her 'rude' when she cannot hear.","Refer gradual hearing loss for audiology.","RACGP"),

I("gp","gp-ckd-acei","General Practice","Chronic disease","moderate","M",62,
 "the GP clinic","type 2 diabetes, BP 152/88, ACR 15 mg/mmol, eGFR 58",
 "asymptomatic",
 "No oedema, fundi background retinopathy.",
 "K 4.6, creatinine 110.",
 "What is the most appropriate first-line reno-protective medicine?",
 "An ACE inhibitor or ARB, titrated, with a check of creatinine and potassium in 1–2 weeks, plus SGLT2 inhibitor as indicated",
 ["NSAID for 'kidney pain' that is not present","High-protein supplements as first-line CKD care","Stopping all antihypertensives because eGFR is 58","Aldosterone antagonist as first-line before ACEI in this setting"],
 "Diabetic CKD with albuminuria: ACEI/ARB and SGLT2i (if eGFR allows), BP target, and avoid NSAIDs.",
 ["NSAIDs worsen CKD.","Protein restriction is nuanced, not high-protein.","BP control is essential.","MRA is add-on in selected albuminuric CKD."],
 "A 20–30% creatinine rise after ACEI can be acceptable — check the guideline, don't panic-stop every 10% blip.","An NSAID for OA in eGFR 30.","Use ACEI/ARB (and SGLT2i) in diabetic albuminuric CKD.","KDIGO / Australian diabetes guidance"),

I("gp","gp-af-anticoag-gp","General Practice","Chronic disease","moderate","F",78,
 "the GP clinic","new AF, rate 88 on a beta blocker, hypertension, no mechanical valve, eGFR 55",
 "no bleeding history, independent",
 "CHA2DS2-VASc 4, HAS-BLED 2.",
 "Echo: no valvular rheumatic MS. Hb 132.",
 "What is the most appropriate stroke-prevention plan?",
 "Start a DOAC (dose-adjusted for age/eGFR/weight) after shared discussion; aspirin is not adequate stroke prevention in AF",
 ["Aspirin 100 mg as equivalent to anticoagulation in AF","No antithrombotic because she is 78","Warfarin as mandatory in all non-valvular AF in 2026","Dual antiplatelet therapy as the AF stroke standard"],
 "Non-valvular AF with CHA2DS2-VASc ≥2: anticoagulation. DOACs are first-line unless valvular (mechanical valve/moderate–severe MS).",
 ["Aspirin is inferior.","Age is a reason for anticoagulation, not against.","Warfarin is for selected indications.","DAPT is for ACS/stents, not AF per se."],
 "Do not use CHA2DS2-VASc to withhold anticoagulation in women by miscounting.","Aspirin 'because she is old'.","Anticoagulate high-risk non-valvular AF with a DOAC.","Heart Foundation / CSANZ"),

I("gp","gp-warfarin-inr","General Practice","Chronic disease","moderate","M",74,
 "the GP clinic","mechanical mitral valve on warfarin, INR 1.6 (target 3.0), no bleeding",
 "routine INR",
 "Well, no melaena, no stroke symptoms.",
 "No interacting antibiotics this week. He missed two doses.",
 "What is the most appropriate action?",
 "Give the missed-dose advice / a one-off extra as per protocol and bring the INR back into range promptly — mechanical mitral valves are high thrombotic risk",
 ["Stop warfarin permanently","Switch to rivaroxaban for a mechanical mitral valve","Aspirin only","Ignore an INR of 1.6 at a mitral mechanical target of 3"],
 "Mechanical mitral valves need warfarin (typically INR 2.5–3.5), not DOACs. Subtherapeutic INR needs active management.",
 ["He needs ongoing anticoagulation.","DOACs are contraindicated for mechanical valves.","Aspirin is adjunct at most.","1.6 is dangerous at this target."],
 "Bridging decisions are specialist — do not leave a mitral valve unprotected.","A DOAC 'because INRs are annoying' on a mechanical valve.","Keep mechanical mitral valves in a therapeutic INR range on warfarin.","CSANZ / ACCP-aligned"),

I("gp","gp-driving-seizure","General Practice","Prevention","moderate","M",26,
 "the GP clinic","first unprovoked seizure 1 week ago, now well, wants to drive to work",
 "no licence restriction discussed in ED",
 "Neurology normal. EEG and MRI pending.",
 "He is a commercial driver.",
 "What is the most appropriate driving advice?",
 "He must not drive until he meets Austroads/state fitness-to-drive criteria; commercial standards are stricter; notify as required",
 ["Drive today if he 'feels fine'","A private car is always fine after any seizure the same week","No need to mention commercial driving","The GP should hide the diagnosis from the patient"],
 "Austroads: seizures have mandatory non-driving periods. Commercial drivers have longer bans. Document the advice.",
 ["Feeling well is not the standard.","Private and commercial differ, but both have bans.","Occupation matters.","He must be told."],
 "Write the driving advice in the notes.","A wink and 'I won't tell Transport'.","Apply Austroads driving standards after seizure.","Austroads Assessing Fitness to Drive"),

I("gp","gp-acd","General Practice","Geriatrics","easy","F",79,
 "the GP clinic","multimorbidity, wants her daughter to decide if she cannot",
 "well today, capacious",
 "No acute illness.",
 "She has not completed an advance care directive.",
 "What is the most appropriate action?",
 "Discuss and help complete an advance care directive and appoint a substitute decision-maker while she has capacity",
 ["Wait until she is delirious in ICU to start the conversation","A family meeting without her as the preferred first step","Assuming doctors can ignore a valid ACD","Telling her ACDs are not recognised in Australia"],
 "Advance care planning is a GP core task. Appointing a medical treatment decision-maker and documenting values is best done when well.",
 ["Delirium is too late for her voice.","She should lead.","Valid ACDs must be respected.","They are legally recognised (details vary by state)."],
 "Ask 'what would a good day look like if you were very unwell?'.","First discussing ceilings of care with the family in the corridor.","Complete advance care planning while the person has capacity.","ACSQHC / state ACD forms"),

I("gp","gp-chlamydia-screen","General Practice","Women's health","easy","F",21,
 "the GP clinic","new partner, asymptomatic, requesting the pill",
 "no discharge, no pain",
 "Cervix normal. Offer screening.",
 "NAAT on urine or self-collected vaginal swab is acceptable.",
 "What is the most appropriate opportunistic test?",
 "Chlamydia (and gonorrhoea) NAAT, plus offer HIV/syphilis as indicated, and discuss safer sex",
 ["No STI tests in asymptomatic young women","CA-125","A Pap smear as the STI test","Treat with metronidazole only without testing"],
 "Opportunistic chlamydia screening in young sexually active people is Australian practice. Self-collection increases uptake.",
 ["Asymptomatic infection causes infertility.","Wrong test.","Cervical screening is not an STI screen.","Wrong empiric."],
 "A contraception visit is an STI-screening visit.","Assuming a regular partner means zero risk.","Offer chlamydia NAAT opportunistically in the young.","Australian STI guidelines"),

I("gp","gp-prep","General Practice","Prevention","moderate","M",29,
 "the GP clinic","HIV-negative, condomless anal sex with multiple partners, wants prevention",
 "well, vaccinated for hepatitis A/B",
 "No contraindication to tenofovir/emtricitabine. eGFR 95.",
 "HIV Ag/Ab negative this week, HBV immune, syphilis negative.",
 "What is the most appropriate prevention?",
 "HIV PrEP (daily or guideline-supported on-demand in eligible MSM) with 3-monthly HIV/STI monitoring",
 ["Post-exposure doxycycline as a substitute for HIV PrEP","Reassurance that Australia has no HIV","Waiting until he is HIV-positive to discuss prevention","Tenofovir alone without a partner drug as standard PrEP"],
 "PrEP is highly effective when adherent. Baseline HIV-negative status is essential. Monitor renal function and STIs.",
 ["DoxyPEP is for bacterial STIs in selected groups, not HIV.","HIV still occurs.","Prevention first.","Standard PrEP is TDF/FTC or TAF/FTC as indicated."],
 "A negative HIV test before starting PrEP is non-negotiable.","Starting PrEP during undiagnosed seroconversion.","Offer PrEP to people at ongoing HIV risk.","ASHM PrEP guidelines"),

I("gp","gp-oa-paracetamol","General Practice","Chronic disease","moderate","F",70,
 "the GP clinic","knee OA, CKD eGFR 38, previous GI bleed",
 "pain limiting shopping",
 "Cool bony knees, no effusion of sepsis.",
 "She has been taking ibuprofen 400 mg tds from the supermarket.",
 "What is the most appropriate analgesic adjustment?",
 "Stop the NSAID; use topical NSAID, paracetamol, and non-drug care; consider a PPI only if an NSAID is unavoidable, and avoid NSAIDs in CKD/GI bleed history if possible",
 ["Continue ibuprofen tds indefinitely","Add naproxen to the ibuprofen","Oral diclofenac as safer in GI bleed history","High-dose codeine as first-line long-term OA therapy"],
 "NSAIDs are effective in OA but dangerous in CKD and previous ulcer. Topical NSAIDs have less systemic risk. Opioids are last-line.",
 ["CKD + NSAID = harm.","Two NSAIDs double harm.","Systemic NSAIDs share GI risk.","Opioids have poor long-term OA evidence."],
 "Ask about supermarket NSAIDs.","Ibuprofen plus an ACEI plus a diuretic (the triple whammy).","Avoid systemic NSAIDs in CKD and prior ulcer.","RACGP OA / Therapeutic Guidelines"),

I("gp","gp-vitamin-d","General Practice","Prevention","moderate","F",82,
 "the GP clinic","housebound, previous fall, dark skin",
 "no bone pain of osteomalacia",
 "Proximal weakness mild. Diet low in fish.",
 "25-OH D 22 nmol/L, Ca 2.15, PTH pending, eGFR 55.",
 "What is the most appropriate action?",
 "Vitamin D replacement and falls/bone assessment; do not use mega-dose stoss therapy without a plan, and do not ignore deficiency in the housebound",
 ["No treatment of a level of 22 nmol/L in an 82-year-old who falls","Annual 600,000 IU as casual unsupervised practice","Calcitriol as first-line in simple dietary/sun deficiency with normal eGFR","Sunbed prescription"],
 "Deficiency is common in housebound older people. Replace cholecalciferol, ensure calcium intake, and assess bones. Very high stoss doses have been linked to falls in some trials — use a measured regimen.",
 ["22 nmol/L is deficient.","Unsupervised mega-doses are not casual care.","Calcitriol is for hypoparathyroidism/advanced CKD, not simple deficiency.","Sunbeds are carcinogenic."],
 "If they never see the sun, they need a supplement, not a lecture.","A one-off 600,000 IU and no follow-up.","Replace vitamin D in deficient housebound older adults.","Healthy Bones Australia / RACGP"),

I("gp","gp-ckd-nsaid-triple","General Practice","Chronic disease","difficult","M",76,
 "the GP clinic","heart failure, on ramipril, frusemide, and recently ibuprofen for gout",
 "rising creatinine, oliguria, dizziness",
 "BP 98/60, dry, no rash of AIN necessarily.",
 "Creatinine 220 (from 110), K 5.8, no obstruction on bladder scan.",
 "What is the most likely iatrogenic cause?",
 "The triple whammy: ACE inhibitor + diuretic + NSAID causing acute kidney injury",
 ["A new obstructive uropathy as the only possibility with an empty bladder","ACE inhibitor allergy with no rash or eosinophilia required","Normal ageing of a doubling of creatinine in a week","A thiazide as the missing culprit when he is on frusemide and ibuprofen"],
 "ACEI/ARB plus diuretic plus NSAID is a classic Australian GP AKI. Stop the NSAID, review volume, and monitor K.",
 ["Bladder is empty.","This is haemodynamic AKI.","A week is acute.","The NSAID is the usual recent add-on."],
 "Write 'no NSAIDs' on the action plan of every ACEI+diuretic patient.","A GP 'just take Nurofen' for gout in HF.","Avoid the ACEI–diuretic–NSAID triple whammy.","KDIGO / NPS MedicineWise"),

I("gp","gp-hf-gp-review","General Practice","Chronic disease","moderate","F",79,
 "the GP clinic","HFrEF, on an ACEI, beta blocker, SGLT2i, and spironolactone, weight up 3 kg in 4 days",
 "orthopnoea, ankle swelling",
 "Bibasal crackles, pitting oedema, BP 112/70, HR 72, no shock.",
 "K 4.4, creatinine 100. She has been eating ham and stopping her frusemide 'because she was going out'.",
 "What is the most appropriate GP action if she is still community-safe?",
 "Restart/increase the loop diuretic, fluid and salt advice, early review, and safety-net for hospital if not diuresing",
 ["Stop the beta blocker immediately in all stable-BP congestion","Add an NSAID for 'leg pain' of oedema","Ignore a 3 kg gain","High-dose prednisolone"],
 "A 2–3 kg gain is congestion. Diuretic flexibility is part of HF action plans. Do not stop prognostic drugs for mild congestion without a reason.",
 ["Beta blockers are prognostic; stop only in shock/severe decompensation.","NSAIDs worsen HF.","Weight is the home sensor.","Steroids cause fluid retention."],
 "Daily weights are the cheapest monitor in HF.","'Just cut the frusemide when you go to a wedding'.","Use a HF action plan for weight gain.","NHFA heart-failure guidelines"),

I("gp","gp-absolute-risk-aboriginal","General Practice","Prevention","moderate","F",47,
 "the Aboriginal Medical Service","Aboriginal woman, smoker, BP 138/86, no prior CVD",
 "annual health check (MBS 715)",
 "BMI 31, no murmur.",
 "Lipids TC 5.8, HDL 1.0, HbA1c 6.0%, eGFR 80. Risk calculators may underestimate in Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people — treat earlier.",
 "What is the most appropriate prevention approach?",
 "An Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander health assessment, earlier CVD-risk treatment thresholds as per Australian guidance, smoking cessation, and culturally safe follow-up",
 ["Using only general-population thresholds without adjustment and delaying care until 75","No smoking advice because it might offend","Skipping the 715 assessment because she looks well","Aspirin as automatic without risk discussion"],
 "Indigenous Australians have CVD events at younger ages. Use recommended age and risk adjustments, and a 715 health check as a structured opportunity.",
 ["Delayed prevention drives the gap.","Respectful advice is expected.","The assessment finds treatable risk.","Aspirin is not automatic."],
 "Start CVD risk conversations younger.","A calculator that ignores ethnicity as an excuse to wait.","Assess and treat CVD risk earlier in Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander adults.","Heart Foundation / NACCHO / RACGP"),

I("gp","gp-otitis-media-child","General Practice","Common presentations","moderate","M",2,
 "the GP clinic","URTI then tugging the right ear, temperature 38.2°C, still drinking",
 "no mastoid swelling, no neck stiffness",
 "Bulging red TM, no perforation yet, well-hydrated.",
 "In Australia, many children can have analgesia and delayed/no antibiotics if mild and over 6 months, but Aboriginal children and under-6-months have different rules.",
 "What is the most appropriate first step for this well child?",
 "Adequate analgesia, then decide on antibiotics using age, severity, laterality, and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander risk of chronic OM",
 ["Aminoglycoside drops through an intact drum as first-line systemic therapy","Immediate mastoidectomy in uncomplicated AOM","No pain relief because 'it is only an ear'","Antihistamines as disease-modifying"],
 "AOM: treat pain first. Antibiotics help a minority; they are more clearly indicated if under 6 months, severe, otorrhoea, or high-risk populations.",
 ["Drops are for otitis externa or selected perforations — not intact-drum AOM as systemic therapy.","No mastoiditis.","Pain is the main symptom.","Antihistamines do not treat AOM."],
 "Look behind the ear every time.","Forgetting analgesia because an antibiotic was written.","Give analgesia and risk-stratify antibiotics in AOM.","Therapeutic Guidelines / OM guidelines for Aboriginal children", age_label="2-year-old"),

I("gp","gp-6-week-hip","General Practice","Prevention","moderate","F",0,
 "the GP clinic","6-week check, first-born, breech at 36 weeks",
 "well, feeding well",
 "Ortolani/Barlow negative today, thigh creases asymmetrical, limited abduction on the left.",
 "Risk factors for DDH: breech, family history, female. Ultrasound is used in infants under 6 months with risk or uncertain exam.",
 "What is the most appropriate hip action?",
 "Refer for hip ultrasound (and paediatric orthopaedics if abnormal) because of breech plus asymmetric exam, even if Ortolani is now negative",
 ["Reassurance that a negative Ortolani at 6 weeks always excludes DDH after breech presentation","X-ray as the best test at 6 weeks (ossification incomplete)","Pavlik harness in GP without imaging","No follow-up of breech babies"],
 "DDH: risk-factor ultrasound, usually around 6 weeks. Clinical exam misses some. X-ray is for older infants when ossification allows.",
 ["Breech is a major risk.","X-ray too early is unhelpful.","Harness is specialist.","Breech babies need a pathway."],
 "Asymmetric creases are a clue, not a diagnosis.","Waiting until walking to refer a breech baby with limited abduction.","Ultrasound hips in high-risk 6-week infants.","RACGP / paediatric orthopaedic guidance", age_label="6-week-old"),

I("gp","gp-pertussis-adult","General Practice","Vaccination","easy","F",32,
 "the GP clinic","32 weeks pregnant, last pertussis vaccine 6 years ago",
 "well, wants to protect the newborn",
 "Pregnancy progressing normally.",
 "Influenza vaccine also indicated in pregnancy.",
 "What is the most appropriate pertussis advice?",
 "A pertussis-containing vaccine in each pregnancy (ideally 20–32 weeks) to protect the neonate by transplacental antibody",
 ["Pertussis vaccine is contraindicated in all pregnancies","Only vaccinate the newborn at birth with dTpa as NIP standard","Antibiotics to the mother as a substitute for vaccination","Delay until postpartum only, as equally protective for the first 6 weeks of infant life"],
 "Maternal dTpa each pregnancy is Australian policy and protects infants too young to be fully vaccinated. Cocooning is extra, not a substitute.",
 ["It is recommended, not contraindicated.","Newborns are not given dTpa at birth on the NIP.","Antibiotics treat disease, not routine prevention.","Antenatal timing is for antibody transfer."],
 "Every pregnancy, even if vaccinated last year.","Waiting until the 6-week infant visit to think about pertussis.","Give pertussis vaccine in each pregnancy.","Australian Immunisation Handbook"),

